nodecode-process-table is a rewrite of Yicheng-Mark's Yicheng-Mark/hermes-windows-plugins:

  https://github.com/Yicheng-Mark/hermes-windows-plugins
  commit 5bad3fb2baf04ce6befecfefce6d14f4c9c98934 (plugin folder windows-process)
  MIT License, Copyright (c) 2026 Yicheng-Mark (LICENSE)

What was rewritten: the one tool, win_process_table, as the tool
process_table; its filters (pid, pids, process, parent, orphans_only,
with_ancestry, include_system, limit); its output shape (each entry's pid,
parent_pid, process, path, started, age_seconds, threads, parent_process,
parent_state, system and the context rows of an ancestry request, over the
counts and the notes); and the parent judgement the plugin exists for --
a parent PID that is gone, or whose number a newer process has taken over.

What changed in the rewrite: the plugin read the Windows process table
through kernel32 via ctypes, and only on Windows. The rewrite keeps the
same judgement and the same answer and reads it on Linux (/proc), macOS and
the BSDs (ps) and Windows (Win32_Process through powershell), so a Nodecode
image on any of the three answers. The 500-row ceiling, the oldest-first
order, the 220-character path cut and the JSON keys are the plugin's.

What was left out: nothing of the plugin's own behaviour. The thread count
is Windows' and Linux's to report, so on macOS and the BSDs it is null;
processes this account cannot open still come back without a path or a
start time, as they did on Windows; and the note that named Windows'
session processes (csrss, wininit, winlogon, services, explorer) is now
worded for the system's own images on any of the three, since only Windows
has those particular names.
